/*
======================================================================
TransactionFraud_GNN LOADING JOBS
======================================================================

One job per exported dataset, 24 of them. Column positions ($0, $1, ...)
map one-to-one onto the PSV columns produced by the matching
tf_gnn_prep.load_* view; sql/postgres/080_create_load_views.sql is the
other half of this contract. Change either side and you change both, and
scripts/check_loader_contracts.py compares them mechanically.

ATTRIBUTE ORDER INSIDE EACH `VALUES` IS THE SCHEMA'S ORDER, not the
PSV's. They agree everywhere except job 22, which appends four endpoint
keys the vertex does not carry. TigerGraph rejects a VALUES list of the
wrong ARITY outright, but a reordering WITHIN a run of same-typed
attributes is accepted silently and writes to the wrong column --- so
regenerate a list from gsql/schema/schema.gsql rather than editing one by
hand.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
DECISIONS ENCODED HERE, so nobody re-litigates them per job
----------------------------------------------------------------------

ONE PSV ROW CREATES FIVE OBJECTS (job 22). The Payment_Transaction
vertex and its Account, Card, Merchant and Location edges all come off
the same line. That is what makes the schema's cardinality contract ---
"exactly one Account and Merchant, at most one Card and Location" --- true
BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by assertion, and it avoids four more copies
of a 27M-row file. Every participation edge takes event_ts_ms and
event_seq from the same line's $2 and $3, which is the premise
tfgnn_validate_graph's edge-stamp check exists to prove.

Transaction_At_Location IS GUARDED. An online merchant has no outlet, so
$27 is empty on those rows. Without the WHERE, TigerGraph would create an
edge to a Merchant_Location whose primary id is the empty string --- a
real vertex, silently, on every card-not-present authorization.

DEVICE AND IP ARE SEPARATE JOBS (23, 24), not more columns on 22. They
are OPTIONAL endpoints with their own row counts: a transaction with no
recorded device produces no row at all. Folding them in would need the
same empty-value guard for a genuinely sparse relation, and would break
the equality between 22's row count and its edge counts that the verifier
relies on.

NO ATTRIBUTE IS A PLACEHOLDER. The previous TF_GNN jobs were mostly `_`
markers for build output --- pagerank, community ids, neighbourhood
aggregates --- that the loader was not allowed to supply. The new schema
declares none of that: "no corpus-wide count, fraud rate, risk score
derived from future labels, embedding, or model output is persisted on an
entity vertex". Every position below carries a real loaded value, and if
a future edit needs a `_` here it is a sign the schema grew a computed
attribute it should not have.

LOAD ORDER IS NOT EXPRESSED IN THIS FILE. It is the dataset numbering in
src/tf_gnn_loader/postgres/export.py, which tigergraph/loading.py walks in
sorted order: all 11 vertex types, then the 10 slowly changing relations,
then the fact table, then the two event-time endpoints. TigerGraph upserts
an unseen endpoint with schema defaults rather than failing, and a vertex
created that way carries first_seen_seq = 0, which passes every
first_seen_seq <= cutoff filter including cutoffs from before the entity
existed. Running these jobs by hand in a different order reintroduces
exactly that.

WHAT NO JOB LOADS, because the schema declares nowhere to put it:
    split_id, causal_fold        separation is a cutoff on event_seq
    error                        an authorization RESPONSE; the scoring
                                 moment is the request
    name, gender, dob            protected attributes
    is_blocked                   a timeless blocklist flag is a function
                                 of the label
    raw email / phone / address  tokenised in PostgreSQL before export
======================================================================
*/

USE GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN


/*
======================================================================
01-11  VERTICES
======================================================================
first_seen_seq is never 0. sql/postgres/030_create_event_sequence.sql
guarantees it and tf_gnn_prep.audit_first_seen gates it, because a zero
stamp makes an entity visible to cutoffs from before it existed.
======================================================================
*/

CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_parties FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX Party VALUES (
      $0,  // party_id
      $1,  // first_seen_seq
      $2,  // first_seen_ts_ms
      $3,  // customer_since_ts_ms
      $4,  // party_type
      $5   // home_country
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_accounts FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX Account VALUES (
      $0,  // account_id
      $1,  // first_seen_seq
      $2,  // first_seen_ts_ms
      $3,  // opened_ts_ms
      $4,  // account_type
      $5,  // base_currency
      $6   // country_code
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_cards FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX Card VALUES (
      $0,  // card_id
      $1,  // first_seen_seq
      $2,  // first_seen_ts_ms
      $3,  // issued_ts_ms
      $4,  // card_type
      $5,  // card_network
      $6,  // card_product
      $7   // is_virtual
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_merchants FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX Merchant VALUES (
      $0,  // merchant_id
      $1,  // first_seen_seq
      $2,  // first_seen_ts_ms
      $3,  // onboarded_ts_ms
      $4,  // merchant_category_code
      $5,  // merchant_country
      $6,  // merchant_type
      $7   // is_online
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_merchant_locations FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  // has_coordinates is the mask and the coordinates are already zeroed
  // when it is false. Never test latitude <> 0 downstream: the prime
  // meridian and the equator are both real, and 0,0 is a place in the
  // Gulf of Guinea.
  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX Merchant_Location VALUES (
      $0,  // location_id
      $1,  // first_seen_seq
      $2,  // first_seen_ts_ms
      $3,  // country_code
      $4,  // region_code
      $5,  // postal_code_prefix
      $6,  // latitude
      $7,  // longitude
      $8   // has_coordinates
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_devices FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  // device_id is an HMAC-SHA256 token, not a device fingerprint.
  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX Device VALUES (
      $0,  // device_id
      $1,  // first_seen_seq
      $2,  // first_seen_ts_ms
      $3,  // device_type
      $4,  // os_family
      $5   // browser_family
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_ip_addresses FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX IP_Address VALUES (
      $0,  // ip_id
      $1,  // first_seen_seq
      $2,  // first_seen_ts_ms
      $3,  // country_code
      $4,  // asn
      $5   // network_type
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_emails FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  // domain is the mail provider and is not personal; the local part
  // never left PostgreSQL.
  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX Email VALUES (
      $0,  // email_id
      $1,  // first_seen_seq
      $2,  // first_seen_ts_ms
      $3   // domain
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_phones FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX Phone VALUES (
      $0,  // phone_id
      $1,  // first_seen_seq
      $2,  // first_seen_ts_ms
      $3,  // country_code
      $4   // line_type
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_addresses FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX Address VALUES (
      $0,  // address_id
      $1,  // first_seen_seq
      $2,  // first_seen_ts_ms
      $3,  // country_code
      $4,  // region_code
      $5   // postal_code_prefix
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_identity_documents FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX Identity_Document VALUES (
      $0,  // document_id
      $1,  // first_seen_seq
      $2,  // first_seen_ts_ms
      $3,  // document_type
      $4   // issuing_country
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


/*
======================================================================
12-21  SLOWLY CHANGING RELATIONS
======================================================================
Every one is (FROM, TO, valid_from_seq, valid_to_seq, confidence,
source_system), so all ten jobs are the same six positions.

VISIBILITY, from the schema:
    valid_from_seq <= s.event_seq
    AND (valid_to_seq == 0 OR s.event_seq < valid_to_seq)

valid_to_seq == 0 means no known end and is the value on nine of the
ten relations: the source never records an ownership or a PII value
being given up.

Account_Has_Card is the exception and the only real tenure in this
schema. Card generations are ordered by the -G<n> reissue suffix, so
generation n's tenure closes exactly where generation n+1's opens, and
"which card was in force at this authorization" is answerable.

WHAT valid_from_seq IS NOT. On the other nine it is an OBSERVABILITY
LOWER BOUND --- max of the two endpoints' first_seen_seq --- because the
source carries no per-attachment time. Do not build an "<x> at onset"
feature on those: under a lower-bound stamp they collapse to the party's
FINAL counts, which is a whole-window leak wearing a point-in-time name,
and the join succeeds and returns a plausible number.
======================================================================
*/

CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_party_owns_account FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Party_Owns_Account VALUES (
      $0,  // FROM Party
      $1,  // TO Account
      $2,  // valid_from_seq
      $3,  // valid_to_seq
      $4,  // confidence
      $5   // source_system
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_account_has_card FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Account_Has_Card VALUES (
      $0,  // FROM Account
      $1,  // TO Card
      $2,  // valid_from_seq
      $3,  // valid_to_seq  --- non-zero on a reissued generation
      $4,  // confidence
      $5   // source_system
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_party_has_email FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Party_Has_Email VALUES ($0, $1, $2, $3, $4, $5)
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_party_has_phone FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Party_Has_Phone VALUES ($0, $1, $2, $3, $4, $5)
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_party_has_address FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Party_Has_Address VALUES ($0, $1, $2, $3, $4, $5)
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_party_has_identity_document FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Party_Has_Identity_Document VALUES ($0, $1, $2, $3, $4, $5)
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_party_has_device FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Party_Has_Device VALUES ($0, $1, $2, $3, $4, $5)
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_party_has_ip FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Party_Has_IP VALUES ($0, $1, $2, $3, $4, $5)
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_party_operates_merchant FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  // Direction is Party -> Merchant. The source register runs the other
  // way (merchant -> proprietor) and the load view flips it.
  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Party_Operates_Merchant VALUES ($0, $1, $2, $3, $4, $5)
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_merchant_has_location FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Merchant_Has_Location VALUES ($0, $1, $2, $3, $4, $5)
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


/*
======================================================================
22  Payment_Transaction AND ITS FOUR PARTICIPATION EDGES
======================================================================
PSV columns:

   0 transaction_id        12 is_recurring
   1 event_time            13 merchant_category_code
   2 event_ts_ms           14 has_event_location
   3 event_seq             15 event_latitude
   4 amount                16 event_longitude
   5 amount_present        17 device_risk_score
   6 currency              18 ip_risk_score
   7 transaction_type      19 fraud_label
   8 channel               20 label_known
   9 entry_mode            21 label_available_seq
  10 card_present          22 label_available_ts_ms
  11 is_cross_border       23 label_source
                           24 account_id   <- endpoint keys, not
                           25 card_id         vertex attributes
                           26 merchant_id
                           27 location_id  <- empty for online merchants

$0-$23 are the vertex in schema order. $24-$27 exist only to create the
edges below; the schema deliberately keeps NO denormalised join keys on
the fact vertex, so none of them is loaded as an attribute.

fraud_label and label_* ARE SUPERVISION METADATA. The schema is explicit
that "no model feature/message query may read them". They load here
because supervision has to come from somewhere; nothing downstream may
treat them as inputs.
======================================================================
*/

CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_transactions FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO VERTEX Payment_Transaction VALUES (
      $0,   // transaction_id
      $1,   // event_time            DATETIME, operator readability only
      $2,   // event_ts_ms           AUTHORITATIVE clock
      $3,   // event_seq             AUTHORITATIVE order
      $4,   // amount
      $5,   // amount_present        separates a real 0.00 from an unloaded amount
      $6,   // currency
      $7,   // transaction_type
      $8,   // channel
      $9,   // entry_mode
      $10,  // card_present
      $11,  // is_cross_border
      $12,  // is_recurring
      $13,  // merchant_category_code
      $14,  // has_event_location
      $15,  // event_latitude
      $16,  // event_longitude
      $17,  // device_risk_score     -1 = unavailable, never 0
      $18,  // ip_risk_score         -1 = unavailable, never 0
      $19,  // fraud_label
      $20,  // label_known
      $21,  // label_available_seq
      $22,  // label_available_ts_ms
      $23   // label_source
    ),

    TO EDGE Transaction_From_Account VALUES (
      $0,   // FROM Payment_Transaction
      $24,  // TO Account --- the initiating/funding account
      $2,   // event_ts_ms
      $3    // event_seq
    ),

    TO EDGE Transaction_Used_Card VALUES (
      $0,   // FROM Payment_Transaction
      $25,  // TO Card
      $2,
      $3
    ),

    TO EDGE Transaction_At_Merchant VALUES (
      $0,   // FROM Payment_Transaction
      $26,  // TO Merchant --- the receiving side
      $2,
      $3
    ),

    // GUARDED. $27 is empty for a card-not-present merchant, which has
    // no outlet. Without the WHERE this creates a Merchant_Location whose
    // primary id is the empty string, on every online authorization.
    TO EDGE Transaction_At_Location VALUES (
      $0,   // FROM Payment_Transaction
      $27,  // TO Merchant_Location
      $2,
      $3
    ) WHERE $27 != ""

    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


/*
======================================================================
23-24  EVENT-TIME ENDPOINTS
======================================================================
The relations the previous loader could not build. cf_Has_Device and
cf_Has_IP are whole-window associations the institution has ON FILE;
these two say which endpoint the AUTHORIZATION came from.

Both stamps come from the transaction's own event_seq / event_ts_ms, not
from the source's edge timestamp: there is exactly one clock, and a
participation edge that disagreed with its own transaction would break
every cutoff-safe query.

Zero rows is legal --- both are optional endpoints --- and
tf_gnn_prep.audit_source_counts reports the count so an empty relation is
a number somebody saw.
======================================================================
*/

CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_transaction_used_device FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Transaction_Used_Device VALUES (
      $0,  // FROM Payment_Transaction
      $1,  // TO Device
      $2,  // event_ts_ms
      $3   // event_seq
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}


CREATE LOADING JOB tfgnn_load_transaction_from_ip FOR GRAPH TransactionFraud_GNN {
  DEFINE FILENAME f;

  LOAD f
    TO EDGE Transaction_From_IP VALUES (
      $0,  // FROM Payment_Transaction
      $1,  // TO IP_Address
      $2,  // event_ts_ms
      $3   // event_seq
    )
    USING SEPARATOR="|";
}
